WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:01.960
Chris Gore, Founder/Owner of FilmThreat/Author/Filmmaker:
Why are 99 percent of movies garbage, well

00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:05.290
you know sometimes they’re garbage movies
are made on purpose.

00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:12.620
Film Courage: Here’s a thought, have movies
lost their cool because television has become

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:13.620
so good?

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:20.150
So if you think back to growing up TV was
was more restricted and everything cool happened

00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:22.310
in movies like Kramer Versus Kramer.

00:00:22.310 --> 00:00:26.670
Oooh…there were some scenes that I wasn't
supposed to see but I saw them anyway right?

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:28.949
Now you can see it all on TV.

00:00:28.949 --> 00:00:37.079
Chris: Yes I do think that television has
succeeded so well when it comes to long-form

00:00:37.079 --> 00:00:38.719
storytelling.

00:00:38.719 --> 00:00:44.809
I do think that there is a bit of abuse in
that where I've seen series that were six

00:00:44.809 --> 00:00:51.179
episodes/six hours that would have made a
really good two-hour movie.

00:00:51.179 --> 00:00:57.449
But I think that there's an appetite for the
serialized long-form storytelling and we've

00:00:57.449 --> 00:00:58.719
become used to that.

00:00:58.719 --> 00:01:05.280
We've become used to the binge watching so
going to the theater to see a story that's

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:11.470
going to wrap itself up in hopefully two hours
maybe is not as satisfying and even when you

00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:16.100
look at the Star Wars films or the Marvel
movies, it really is just every Marvel movie

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:19.740
is a continuation of the last Marvel movie
in some form because we know there's going

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:20.740
to be easter eggs.

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:24.930
We know there's going to be cameos from characters.

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:33.520
So Marvel movies in a sense have become not
only its own genre but sort of a form of long-form

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:42.590
storytelling with the Disney plus series the
those kind of filling in the blanks between

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:44.390
movies.

00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:45.780
But I think that television is great.

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:49.979
I mean television is where no clothes in movies
has migrated.

00:01:49.979 --> 00:01:53.390
There's hardly people without clothing in
films anymore.

00:01:53.390 --> 00:02:00.340
I mean you're going sacrifice something at
the box office if you choose to include grown

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:05.750
up themes in your feature film being released
to theaters, if there's birthday suits in

00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:10.450
it that has migrated onto television.

00:02:10.450 --> 00:02:15.750
you know it's Mr. Skin [AuthorJim McBride]
who was recently on the Film Threat podcast

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:24.120
told me it's a golden age of birthday suits
in television, not so much at the movies.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:31.240
There aren't like the genre of teen whoopie
comedy kind of doesn't even exist.

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:40.380
People forget that the original Porky's film
[1981] such as it was it does feature people

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:47.780
without clothing but also there's an underlying
message of anti-semitism woven within that

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:51.740
film that of course no one remembers except
for me, I remember this.

00:02:51.740 --> 00:03:01.440
I don't know why I remember weird facts but
maybe this is also why television, part of

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:08.020
the reason that television in a way is more
popular than than films I don't know if anyone's

00:03:08.020 --> 00:03:13.440
ever done this study I would love to see because
I think box office isn't a relevant metric

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:16.100
by which to look at.

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:21.290
It's something the industry uses as a piece
of marketing I don't like that box office

00:03:21.290 --> 00:03:22.360
is so important.

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:25.670
It tends to be to Hollywood because it's again
a piece of marketing.

00:03:25.670 --> 00:03:29.820
My mother who doesn't pay attention to the
industry whatsoever she knows what the number

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:35.980
one movie at the box office is because it's
used in the marketing of movies right.

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:41.320
But that's not an important number because
that number is irrelevant because due to inflation

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:45.380
if adjusted to inflation and you can look
this up Gone With The Wind [1939] is in the

00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:56.370
top films not whatever the purported top films
are right.

00:03:56.370 --> 00:03:59.710
Because we're not privy to a lot of the data
from these streaming services.

00:03:59.710 --> 00:04:06.090
We really don't know what is incredibly popular
but they wouldn't be making so many of these

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:11.930
series if they weren't at least getting numbers
that got the eyeballs they needed to be somewhat

00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:23.340
profitable but because we as the general public
are not privy to those numbers, other than

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:28.560
the people who have access to that data, we're
not able to learn the lessons from that.

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:33.430
There are lessons to be learned about the
failure of something like Terminator: Dark

00:04:33.430 --> 00:04:34.430
Fate [2019].

00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:36.880
Right there's a lesson to be learned.

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:46.100
I don't think Hollywood's quite learned the
lesson yet.

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:47.990
That's kind of how we learn right?

00:04:47.990 --> 00:04:55.480
You see what resonates with an audience and
box office is one barometer of that adjusted

00:04:55.480 --> 00:04:59.790
to inflation I think is the better way to
report those numbers.

00:04:59.790 --> 00:05:05.650
Film Courage: Do you have more hope for the
future of television or film?

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:09.840
Chris: I certainly have hope for the future
of television.

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:18.180
There are I think that statistically speaking
if I watch something on television and I vet

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:25.250
that choice the idea that I'll have a good
experience with a show based on recommendations

00:05:25.250 --> 00:05:31.990
(friend recommendations) tends to hit more
often than not with the movies, not so much

00:05:31.990 --> 00:05:32.990
with the movies.

00:05:32.990 --> 00:05:35.190
It's probably 50/50 or less.

00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:42.580
There will be a decent experience if I know
what I'm getting into I am a person that does

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:44.371
enjoy a good bad film.

00:05:44.371 --> 00:05:46.660
Film Courage: Why?

00:05:46.660 --> 00:05:54.620
Chris: Good/bad films are just I mean look
there's a certain kind of television that

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:58.300
is made for stoners right.

00:05:58.300 --> 00:06:05.910
I think a show like Archer (that’s the animated
show) Archer that's like stoner tv.

00:06:05.910 --> 00:06:10.900
I mean most of the Adult Swim shows are for
stoners right?

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:13.240
They're sort of a good/bad.

00:06:13.240 --> 00:06:15.230
I mean like Tim &amp; Eric [Tim Heidecker and
Eric Wareheim] their show.

00:06:15.230 --> 00:06:22.050
It's kind of based on that and there are films
I tend to like but I know that they're bad

00:06:22.050 --> 00:06:32.760
Troma movies by Lloyd Kaufman one of the longest
existing independent film studios is Troma

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:33.760
Studios.

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:42.290
What you're getting into you also should be
aware that James Gunn from The Suicide Squad

00:06:42.290 --> 00:06:47.360
and The Guardians Of The Galaxy franchise,
he began his career at Troma Studios, he wrote

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:54.500
Tromeo and Juliet [1996] so I know it's going
to be bad, it's not like something that's

00:06:54.500 --> 00:07:01.070
going to change my life but I will laugh and
I don't have to be fully engaged in it except

00:07:01.070 --> 00:07:05.910
for maybe the scenes where there's lots of
gore or violence which you can always count

00:07:05.910 --> 00:07:13.770
on in a Troma movie but that's the place where
a lot of people cut their teeth is in the

00:07:13.770 --> 00:07:16.430
exploitation genre.

00:07:16.430 --> 00:07:25.340
Everyone from James Gunn, Francis Ford Coppola,
Ron Howard who famously worked for Roger Corman,

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:34.350
it's a place you can play and it's okay to
fail and to learn the basics.

00:07:34.350 --> 00:07:39.780
I think it's just as important to watch a
bad film and take the lessons from it as it

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:43.210
is to watch a good film.

00:07:43.210 --> 00:07:51.600
So why are 99 percent of movies garbage well
sometimes garbage movies are made on purpose.

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:53.770
There's a big business in that.

00:07:53.770 --> 00:08:02.060
Just ask Lloyd Kaufman or Charlie Band Full
Moon Entertainment famously done most famously

00:08:02.060 --> 00:08:10.500
known for the Puppet Master series (love the
Puppet Master films) they're dumb fun.

00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:16.690
I think it's kind of like just knowing what
you you know you can't eat steak for dinner

00:08:16.690 --> 00:08:19.500
at every meal.

00:08:19.500 --> 00:08:24.770
Sometimes fast food or quick meals are going
to work their way in and that's what I consider

00:08:24.770 --> 00:08:26.230
a bad movie.

00:08:26.230 --> 00:08:32.440
I just want to know I'm getting one, unlike
Terminator: Dark Fate where I go into every

00:08:32.440 --> 00:08:38.769
movie hopeful going to every movie it's going
to be awesome and it's not.

00:08:38.769 --> 00:08:46.369
Film Courage: When an artist wins a Razzie,
how much of it do you think was actually planned

00:08:46.369 --> 00:08:47.369
or none of it?

00:08:47.369 --> 00:08:49.170
I mean does anyone really want to win?

00:08:49.170 --> 00:08:54.360
Chris: I don't know that anyone ever wants
to win a Razzie [a Golden Raspberry Award].

00:08:54.360 --> 00:08:58.670
I mean Showgirls [1995] won a Razzie.

00:08:58.670 --> 00:09:06.069
I mean that film actually we look at now there's
so much more going on in that film Showgirls.

00:09:06.069 --> 00:09:09.440
There are bad movies that age differently.

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:13.600
Maybe they were ahead of its time, they're
ahead of their time like a film like the original

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:18.949
Blade Runner [1982] or a film like Showgirls
that there was much more going on beneath

00:09:18.949 --> 00:09:20.339
the surface.

00:09:20.339 --> 00:09:29.510
I mean the trans community in particular has
embraced the movie Showgirls as just having

00:09:29.510 --> 00:09:30.880
much deeper meaning.

00:09:30.880 --> 00:09:35.430
There's a documentary that discusses this
that I highly recommend that you check out

00:09:35.430 --> 00:09:38.519
called you You Don’t Nomi [2020].

00:09:38.519 --> 00:09:39.740
Check out that documentary.

00:09:39.740 --> 00:09:48.449
That's a whole deep dive analysis of Showgirls,
a sort of re-examination of that.

00:09:48.449 --> 00:09:54.529
Additionally, like why there's a whole fandom
around Showgirls which I find fascinating

00:09:54.529 --> 00:10:01.740
great documentary that breaks that apart and
looks at it but we just don't know what's

00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:03.860
going to age well with time.

00:10:03.860 --> 00:10:08.509
What's going to defy, what will be a cult
classic which no filmmaker wants to win a

00:10:08.509 --> 00:10:13.660
Razzie or have a film that's called a “cult
classic,” because both of those things imply

00:10:13.660 --> 00:10:21.899
that you failed and I don't think I've ever
met a filmmaker that tried to make a bad movie.

00:10:21.899 --> 00:10:26.490
Although I will say this, John Waters has
a great quote about that.

00:10:26.490 --> 00:10:30.270
John Waters said if you try to make a bad
film you can't fail.

00:10:30.270 --> 00:10:31.270
Question For The Viewers:

00:10:31.270 --> 00:10:31.276
What is your favorite bad movie?

